WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:14.004
GHOST SHORT · HOW TO APPROACH
Blown fuse: find the cause first
The circuit: 5 V on J1 → fuse F1 (0.5 A) → power LED D1 (via R3) and a divider R1/R2 that must put 2.5 V on TP1.
It came back with F1 blown and R2 missing.
You will learn: why a blown fuse is a symptom, checking for a short before powering up, and finding a short that is not a part.

2
00:00:19.139 --> 00:00:33.790
STEP 1 · THE FUSE
Power off, Ω across the fuse: OL. F1 is open.
Why: A fuse is a deliberate weak link that melts above its rating, here 0.5 A. Something drew that much current, and a new fuse fitted blindly would blow again.

3
00:00:33.791 --> 00:00:48.432
STEP 2 · BOTH SIDES OF THE FUSE
J1's + pin to ground (TP2): OL. But F1 is open, so this only covers J1 and the trace up to the fuse.
Why: The open fuse splits the board in two. Measure each half to ground on its own.

4
00:00:48.433 --> 00:01:00.945
STEP 2 · BOTH SIDES OF THE FUSE
F1's output side to ground: 0.0 Ω. The fused +5 V is shorted to ground.
Why: Only a short after the fuse sends its current through the fuse: that is what blew it. The loads draw milliamps, so a healthy +5 V reads kilohms here.

5
00:01:00.946 --> 00:01:13.134
STEP 3 · NARROW IT DOWN
No part on +5 V can read 0 Ω: R1 is 10 kΩ, D1 sits behind R3, and R2 is missing.
Why: When every part on the net is ruled out, the short is in the copper itself.

6
00:01:13.135 --> 00:01:25.902
STEP 3 · NARROW IT DOWN
Follow the +5 V trace from the fuse and look at the copper, where a ground trace runs beside it.
Why: Shorts are not always parts: a sliver of copper left over from etching, a whisker of solder or a stray wire strand can join two nets.

7
00:01:25.903 --> 00:01:37.906
STEP 4 · MISSING PART
One more: R2's footprint is empty. The divider gives V = 5 V × R2 / (R1 + R2), and R1 is 10 kΩ.
Why: Without R2, TP1 would sit at the full 5 V. Work out which R2 gives 2.5 V.

8
00:01:37.912 --> 00:01:51.913
WHAT YOU LEARNED
Never just replace the fuse
A blown fuse is a symptom: measure Ω to ground on both sides of it before fitting a new one.
Only a short after the fuse can blow it: the open fuse tells you which half of the board to search.
When no part can explain a short, inspect the copper: slivers, whiskers, bridges.
